Resolution: SSE streaming pattern for AI chat (acad-abl.4)

## Question

What pattern should the client use to consume the AI turn stream (text/event-stream) with TanStack Query 5?

## Answer (recommended pattern)

Use one feature-local stream reader built on raw `fetch` plus `response.body.getReader()`, parsed with a small spec-shaped SSE parser, exposed as an `AsyncGenerator` of typed frames. Trigger it with `useMutation` (the stream starts from a POST, which is a command, not a query). Per frame, write incremental state into a zustand store in the feature (`src/features/plan-conversations/stores/`). On terminal frames, invalidate the affected React Query caches so canonical server state replaces the ephemeral stream state.

No new dependencies. The frame format in the contract is plain `event:` + `data:` pairs, so a parser of about 40 lines covers it. If edge cases appear later (BOM, CRLF, multiline `data:`), ask approval for `eventsource-parser` (maintained by rexxars/Vercel, provides `createParser` and `EventSourceParserStream`, buffers partial lines across chunk boundaries). Source: https://github.com/rexxars/eventsource-parser

## Why the obvious alternatives lose

- Native `EventSource`: GET only, no request body, no custom headers. The turn endpoint requires a POST with a JSON body. Source: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/EventSource
- The shared axios client (`src/lib/api-client.ts`): the browser adapter uses XMLHttpRequest. Axios cannot expose a progressive response stream in the browser; `responseType: 'stream'` is a Node.js concept, and browsers get only `onDownloadProgress`. Source: https://github.com/axios/axios
- `@microsoft/fetch-event-source`: supports POST, headers, body, and AbortController, but the Azure repo is effectively unmaintained (51 open issues, 23 open PRs, no releases). Not worth adopting. Source: https://github.com/Azure/fetch-event-source
- TanStack Query `experimental_streamedQuery`: a queryFn helper that accumulates an AsyncIterable into an array. It is experimental, query-shaped (refetch and retry semantics), and fits a pull stream, not a one-shot POST-triggered turn. Source: https://tanstack.com/query/latest/docs/framework/react/reference/streamedQuery

## Contract facts (from /home/shehab/Projects/academic-advisor-backend/openapi.json)

POST `/api/plan-conversations/{conversation}/turns` (operationId `createPlanConversationTurn`), body `{ "message": string, max 10000 }`, responds 200 `text/event-stream`. Frame format is `event: <type>\ndata: <json>\n\n`. `seq` increments by one across every frame of a turn; `turn_id` is constant per turn. Frames:

1. `token`: `{"turn_id", "seq", "token"}`. Concatenate `token` values in `seq` order to rebuild the assistant message.
2. `tool.applied`: `{"turn_id", "seq", "tool", "payload"}`. The advisor applied a plan mutation (example tool `plan.add_course`).
3. `submit.suggested`: `{"turn_id", "seq"}`. The model asked to submit but the conversation is not armed.
4. `submit.result`: `{"turn_id", "seq", "status": "submitted", "plan_id"}` or `{"turn_id", "seq", "status": "blocked", "errors": {"allowance": [..]}}`. Only for an armed conversation.
5. `turn.completed`: `{"turn_id", "seq", "title"}`. Terminal success. `title` is null when the conversation keeps its existing title.
6. `error`: `{"turn_id", "seq", "code", "retryable", "key", "message"}`. Terminal failure. `code` is `turn_failed` or `turn_timeout`. Example key `advisor.turn_failed`.

The stream ends after `turn.completed` or `error`. Before streaming, the endpoint can return JSON errors: 401, 403, 404, 422, and 429 with `QuotaExceededError` `{ "message", "key": "advisor.quota_exhausted" }`.

POST `/api/plan-conversations/{conversation}/submit-arm` (operationId `armPlanConversationSubmission`): arms the submission flag, streams one `submit.armed` frame `{"conversation_id", "submission_confirmed_at"}`, then ends the stream. Errors 401/403/404. The same reader serves it; treat it as a plain mutation whose promise resolves after the single frame.

## Code sketch

```ts
// src/features/plan-conversations/api/stream-turn.ts
import { env } from '@/config/env';

export type TurnFrame =
  | { event: 'token'; turnId: string; seq: number; token: string }
  | { event: 'tool.applied'; turnId: string; seq: number; tool: string; payload: unknown }
  | { event: 'submit.suggested'; turnId: string; seq: number }
  | { event: 'submit.result'; turnId: string; seq: number; status: 'submitted' | 'blocked'; planId?: number; errors?: Record<string, string[]> }
  | { event: 'turn.completed'; turnId: string; seq: number; title: string | null }
  | { event: 'error'; turnId: string; seq: number; code: 'turn_failed' | 'turn_timeout'; retryable: boolean; key: string; message: string };

// Yields one raw SSE event object per frame: { event, data }.
async function* sseEvents(body: ReadableStream<Uint8Array>): AsyncGenerator<{ event: string; data: string }> {
  const reader = body.getReader();
  const decoder = new TextDecoder();
  let buffer = '';

  while (true) {
    const { done, value } = await reader.read();
    if (done) break;
    buffer += decoder.decode(value, { stream: true }); // handles UTF-8 splits across chunks
    let index: number;
    while ((index = buffer.indexOf('\n\n')) !== -1) {
      const block = buffer.slice(0, index);
      buffer = buffer.slice(index + 2);
      let event = 'message';
      const dataLines: string[] = [];
      for (const line of block.split('\n')) {
        if (line.startsWith('event:')) event = line.slice(6).trim();
        else if (line.startsWith('data:')) dataLines.push(line.slice(5).trimStart());
      }
      if (dataLines.length) yield { event, data: dataLines.join('\n') };
    }
  }
}

export async function* streamTurn(
  conversation: number,
  message: string,
  signal?: AbortSignal,
): AsyncGenerator<TurnFrame> {
  const response = await fetch(`${env.API_URL}/plan-conversations/${conversation}/turns`, {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json', Accept: 'text/event-stream' },
    credentials: 'include',
    body: JSON.stringify({ message }),
    signal,
  });

  // Pre-stream JSON failures: 401, 403, 404, 422, 429 (quota). Discriminate on content-type.
  if (!response.ok || !response.headers.get('content-type')?.includes('text/event-stream')) {
    const json = await response.json().catch(() => null);
    throw new ApiError({ status: response.status, message: json?.message, key: json?.key });
  }

  for await (const { event, data } of sseEvents(response.body!)) {
    yield parseTurnFrame(event, JSON.parse(data) as TurnFrame); // zod validate, keep types in one schema module
  }
}
```

```ts
// src/features/plan-conversations/hooks/use-send-turn.ts
export const useSendTurn = (conversation: number) => {
  const queryClient = useQueryClient();
  const controllerRef = useRef<AbortController | null>(null);

  const mutation = useMutation({
    mutationFn: async (message: string) => {
      const controller = new AbortController();
      controllerRef.current = controller;
      const store = useTurnStreamStore.getState(); // zustand, keyed by conversation

      for await (const frame of streamTurn(conversation, message, controller.signal)) {
        switch (frame.event) {
          case 'token': store.appendToken(conversation, frame); break;
          case 'tool.applied':
            // The plan changed server side. Refresh it live.
            void queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ['plan'] });
            break;
          case 'submit.suggested': store.setSubmitSuggested(conversation, true); break;
          case 'submit.result': store.setSubmitResult(conversation, frame); break;
          case 'turn.completed':
            store.complete(conversation, frame);
            void queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ['plan-conversations', conversation] });
            break;
          case 'error':
            store.fail(conversation, frame);
            break;
        }
      }
    },
    onError: (error) => { /* notification via useNotifications, keep partial text visible */ },
  });

  return { ...mutation, stop: () => controllerRef.current?.abort() };
};
```

Keep the terminal handling inside the loop (the `error` frame is data, not a thrown failure), and let `turn.completed` be the only success path that invalidates caches.

## Cancellation behavior

- One `AbortController` per turn, held in a ref. Pass its `signal` to `fetch`.
- Unmount does not abort a TanStack Query mutation. Mutations keep running after the observer unmounts, so wire abort explicitly: call `stop()` from a stop button, and abort in a `useEffect` cleanup if the design drops the stream on navigation. Source: https://tanstack.com/query/latest/docs/framework/react/guides/mutations
- On abort, `fetch` and `reader.read()` reject with `AbortError`. Check `signal.aborted` (or `error.name === 'AbortError'`) and swallow it as a normal outcome. Keep the partial text in the store and mark it stopped; the contract has no resume support, so a stopped turn stays partial. Source: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/ReadableStream
- `AbortSignal.timeout()` is a cheap guard for a hung stream if the product wants one.

## Error and mid-stream failure handling

- Pre-stream: any non-200 or JSON content-type means the stream never started. Parse the JSON body and surface it (401 already redirects globally via the axios client, but this call bypasses axios, so replicate the 401 redirect or route through a shared helper). Map 429 to a quota-exhausted notice with key `advisor.quota_exhausted`.
- In-stream `error` frame: terminal by contract. Do not reconnect. Show `message`, keep partial tokens, and enable retry when `retryable` is true. Retry is a new POST (the endpoint records a user message per turn), never a stream resume.
- Network break mid-stream (`TypeError` from `reader.read()`): also terminal. The frames carry `seq` for client-side ordering only; there is no `id:` field and no `Last-Event-ID` resume support, so EventSource-style auto-reconnect does not apply. Source: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/EventSource
- Do not opt into mutation retries (`retry` defaults to 0, confirmed in the mutations guide). A retried POST would double-apply the user message. Source: https://tanstack.com/query/latest/docs/framework/react/guides/mutations
- Validate `seq` increments and `turn_id` stability; drop and log violations instead of rendering garbled text.

## MSW 2 mocking

Use `http.post` handlers that return an `HttpResponse` whose body is a `ReadableStream` with header `content-type: text/event-stream`. MSW 2 supports any web stream body, and its docs show exactly this streaming shape. Sources: https://mswjs.io/docs/http/mocking-responses/streaming , https://github.com/mswjs/msw/discussions/1769

```ts
// src/testing/mocks/handlers/plan-conversations.ts
const sse = (frames: Array<{ event: string; data: unknown }>, gapMs = 20) =>
  new HttpResponse(
    new ReadableStream({
      start(controller) {
        const encoder = new TextEncoder();
        frames.forEach((frame, i) => {
          setTimeout(() => {
            controller.enqueue(encoder.encode(`event: ${frame.event}\ndata: ${JSON.stringify(frame.data)}\n\n`));
            if (i === frames.length - 1) controller.close();
          }, gapMs * (i + 1));
        });
      },
    }),
    { headers: { 'content-type': 'text/event-stream' } },
  );

http.post(`${env.API_URL}/plan-conversations/:conversation/turns`, () =>
  sse([
    { event: 'token', data: { turn_id: T, seq: 1, token: 'Hel' } },
    { event: 'token', data: { turn_id: T, seq: 2, token: 'lo' } },
    { event: 'turn.completed', data: { turn_id: T, seq: 3, title: null } },
  ]),
);
```

Facts verified against the installed package (msw 2.15.0 in this repo):

- MSW's first-class `sse` namespace (docs: https://mswjs.io/docs/sse/) is aimed at `EventSource` clients and shipped as a public API in MSW 3.0.0 (published 2026-09-28, npm `msw time`). msw 2.15.0 contains the internals (`node_modules/msw/lib/core/sse.mjs`) but exposes no public `./sse` export; `msw/browser` exports only `setupWorker`. So on MSW 2 the `http` + ReadableStream handler is the supported route, and it is also the more faithful test: it exercises the client's real parser over the wire format.
- The `sse` namespace intercepts EventSource connections, not fetch streams (blog: https://mswjs.io/blog/server-sent-events-are-here).
- Testing notes: vitest runs in jsdom (`environment: 'jsdom'` in vite.config.ts) with `server.listen({ onUnhandledRequest: 'error' })` in `src/testing/setup-tests.ts`. Node supplies `ReadableStream`, `TextEncoder`, and `TextDecoder` under vitest, so the handler works as written. In tests, flush with `await vi.waitFor(...)` or `findBy*` queries rather than sleeps.
- Mock the error paths the same handler file: return `HttpResponse.json({ message, key: 'advisor.quota_exhausted' }, { status: 429 })` for quota, and an `error` terminal frame for `turn_failed`.

## Runner-up

Runner-up: keep everything in the React Query cache. `useMutation` reads the same stream, but each frame calls `queryClient.setQueryData(['plan-conversations', conversation, 'stream'], updater)`, and the UI subscribes with `useQuery`. This is the common community pattern for ChatGPT-style clients. Source: https://tanstack.com/query/latest/docs/framework/react/reference/classes/QueryClient . It loses because in-flight frames are request-scoped ephemeral state, not server state; merging partial frames into the cache invites stale-partial bugs on retry and navigation, and the durable result still needs invalidation. Pick it only if the team wants one state system and accepts a dedicated, never-persisted stream query key per turn.

